From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server, ntdll, advapi32: running a program as another account

CreateProcessWithLogonW was a stub that failed. CreateProcessWithTokenW and
CreateProcessAsUser started the program as the caller, under the other
account's Windows token: on disk it was still the caller.

Each process of a shared prefix runs as its Unix account, so a program for
another account has to be started as that account. Now the elevation
broker's root monitor starts it (sg-session 0.1.0-192):

- A new server request, make_launch_ticket, makes a one-time launch ticket
  for a token's account. It does so only for SYSTEM (CreateProcessAsUser:
  SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege, so a service can start a program on a
  logged-on user's desktop with WTSQueryUserToken's token), or for the
  caller LogonUser gave that token to. Never for root, SYSTEM or another
  system account (below 1000).
- advapi32 sends the ticket, the program and its arguments, and the
  working directory to the broker ("@launch"). The broker's root monitor
  starts the program as that account, with an environment made from the
  account alone, never the caller's.
- The new process names its ticket when it starts (register_launch, from
  ntdll). claim_launch then gives the caller handles to the process and its
  first thread, with full access. CreateProcessWithLogonW and friends wait
  for that and return PROCESS_INFORMATION; the exit code works as usual.
- CreateProcessWithLogonW logs on with LogonUser (1709). With
  LOGON_NETCREDENTIALS_ONLY it starts the program as the caller.
- A program for the caller's own account takes the usual path.
  advapi32's CreateProcessAsUserW is now its own function, no longer a
  forward to kernel32.

The started program's environment, creation flags and startup information
are its account's own. Its display is the account's own session's, when the
account is logged on. Showing a program on the caller's display for an
account that is not logged on is still to come.

Gate: test/runasuser-gate.sh (sgsystem, sgtest1, sgtest2, the broker as
root; SG_SESSION_SRC). Broker mutants SG_MUTANT_LAUNCH_ANY_REQUESTER and
SG_MUTANT_SPAWN_SYSTEM_ACCOUNTS (SG_BROKER_CFLAGS).
